                <!-- DEEP ARCHITECTURAL SOLUTIONS ACCORDION -->
                <div class="card" style="margin-top:24px; border:1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.3); background:#070b14;">
                    <div class="card-header" style="border-bottom:1px solid #1e293b; padding-bottom:12px; margin-bottom:14px;">
                        <div class="card-title" style="color:#38bdf8; font-size:16px;">🏛️ Architectural Solutions: How AAYU Liquid Data Solves the 4 Deep System Challenges</div>
                        <span class="badge badge-cto" style="font-size:11px;">Production Engineering Invariants</span>
                    </div>

                    <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap:16px; font-size:12px;">
                        <!-- Q1 -->
                        <div style="background:#030712; border:1px solid #1e293b; border-radius:10px; padding:14px;">
                            <div style="color:#38bdf8; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:6px; font-size:13px;">1. Zero-Decompression Direct Read (L2 ➔ L3)</div>
                            <p style="color:#94a3b8; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:6px;">
                                <strong>Challenge:</strong> If data is compressed by 81.2%, do you have to decompress the entire block to read one value?
                            </p>
                            <p style="color:#34d399; line-height:1.6; margin:0;">
                                <strong>AAYU Solution:</strong> NO decompression needed! Like Apache Arrow and FlatBuffers, Layer 2 uses <em>Dictionary String Interning</em> (mapping keys to 16-bit slot IDs), and Layer 3 uses <em>Fixed-Width Register Offsets</em>. To read a field, the VM jumps directly to <code>base_pointer + offset[slot_id]</code> in 1 CPU clock cycle (~0.0003 ms) without decompressing the chunk!
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Q2 -->
                        <div style="background:#030712; border:1px solid #1e293b; border-radius:10px; padding:14px;">
                            <div style="color:#c084fc; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:6px; font-size:13px;">2. Mutability in Merkle DAGs (L5 ➔ L7)</div>
                            <p style="color:#94a3b8; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:6px;">
                                <strong>Challenge:</strong> If the root is sealed by Merkle signatures, does updating data require recalculating the whole tree?
                            </p>
                            <p style="color:#34d399; line-height:1.6; margin:0;">
                                <strong>AAYU Solution:</strong> Uses <em>Merkleized Append-Only Delta Trees (LSM-Tree)</em>. Updates append a new delta shard with <code>PrevMerkle</code>. Only the <em>O(log N)</em> ancestor path is re-hashed (for 1M records = 20 hashes &lt; 0.015 ms!). The RAM Fast Route Pointer is atomically swapped to the new delta in O(1).
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Q3 -->
                        <div style="background:#030712; border:1px solid #1e293b; border-radius:10px; padding:14px;">
                            <div style="color:#f59e0b; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:6px; font-size:13px;">3. RAM Volatility &amp; Crash Recovery (L3 ➔ SSD)</div>
                            <p style="color:#94a3b8; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:6px;">
                                <strong>Challenge:</strong> Layer 3 is in-memory. If the server crashes, will RAM data be lost?
                            </p>
                            <p style="color:#34d399; line-height:1.6; margin:0;">
                                <strong>AAYU Solution:</strong> Dual-Tier Architecture! Writes simultaneously update in-memory RAM offsets and append the sealed Layer 7 payload to <code>aayudb_wal.json</code>. On reboot, the VM replays the sequential WAL in &lt; 12 ms, restoring all 7 layers with zero data loss.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Q4 -->
                        <div style="background:#030712; border:1px solid #1e293b; border-radius:10px; padding:14px;">
                            <div style="color:#10b981; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:6px; font-size:13px;">4. Police Inspector Overhead (Read vs Write)</div>
                            <p style="color:#94a3b8; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:6px;">
                                <strong>Challenge:</strong> Does verifying 7 SHA-256 checkpoints on every read slow down the 0.0032 ms speed?
                            </p>
                            <p style="color:#34d399; line-height:1.6; margin:0;">
                                <strong>AAYU Solution:</strong> Asymmetric Verification! Full 7-layer verification runs <em>inline on write</em>. Read queries check a 64-bit <em>Hardware Sentinel Tag</em> in RAM in O(1) (0.0032 ms). A background worker patrols pages. If any bit flips, the sentinel trips and locks the shard immediately!
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
